From 0af588212f51652b5200c5f10b00d127487d78b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 12:13:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build the back office: enquiries, orders, catalogue, users
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Six links in the sidebars led to 404s. They lead somewhere now, and the
studio can run the business without going near the database.

Enquiries arrive in a list you can filter, open to read in full, quote
against, and turn into an order. Quoting tells the customer, and an enquiry
that already became an order refuses to become a second one.

Orders get a list filtered by status and payment, and a detail screen for
status, designer, production notes and the internal notes a customer never
sees. Moving an order to shipped or delivered tells them; a designer swap
doesn't interrupt anyone. Payments taken elsewhere — a bank transfer, the
card machine, cash at the counter — are recorded against the order as real
payment rows rather than a status that changed by itself.

Products and portfolio pieces can be created, edited, photographed and
hidden. Photographs go to R2 and the site shows them, which it never did
before: every image slot rendered a placeholder whatever was uploaded. What
is stored is the object key, not a signed URL — a URL would expire and leave
a product with a broken picture ten minutes later. Sizes are retired rather
than deleted, because orders and prices point at them.

Users can be invited, promoted and suspended. Nobody is ever sent a password
somebody else chose: the account is created with one nobody knows and they
set their own from an emailed link. Two rules stop a lock-out — you can't
strip your own admin access, and the last administrator can't be demoted or
suspended. Changing a role ends that person's sessions, since the role
decides which portal they land in.

The studio gets its own order list and a form for raising work that came in
by phone or at the counter, where the price typed is the price that stands.

Saved items can finally be saved: there was a page for them and no way to
put anything on it.

The dashboard's placeholder promising revenue figures is replaced by the
figures — money taken by month, what is still to collect, the enquiry
pipeline — and says plainly that payment isn't live until the keys are set.

One accessibility fix worth naming: a <label> wrapped round a <select> takes
its accessible name from the option list, so those fields announced as "Role
Customer Designer Proofreader Administrator". They are associated by id now,
through a shared SelectField.

